        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-16T20:22:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m a little confused about the difference between MtlPietRenderer and skgpu::piet::MtlRenderer. On both things we\u0027re accessing metal specific things, so what are we gaining from them being two classes?\n\nLike could we just move all the guts of skgpu::piet::RendererBase/MtlRenderer to just be part of PietRenderer and MtlPientRenderer?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 32,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 32,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "771425230258e0ba651cd47e1befa99048df569e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4f554cf1_cbdafded",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/piet/PietRenderer_Metal.mm",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 32,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T18:57:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The distinction is that skgpu::piet::Renderer (along with all other types in src/gpu/piet) is purely intended to be a C++ wrapper around the pgpu C library type and related free functions, whereas skgpu::graphite::PietRenderer is meant to bridge graphite types (such as SharedContext, Texture, CommandBuffer) with the former.\n\nThe skgpu::piet::* definitions handle things like automatically calling piet library destructors, wrapping around piet path iterators, etc without directly depending on graphite types. I think it makes sense to maintain that separation (between src/gpu/piet and src/graphite/Piet* types).\n\nOne complication here is the approach I took to provide some strongly typed piet API wrappers where piet consumes backend API types directly as `void*`. `skgpu::piet::MtlRenderer` is only there to declare a typed interface in terms of `id\u003cMTLDevice\u003e, id\u003cMTLCommandQueue\u003e` etc and do so near the pgpu C API boundary. This type enforcement is technically already done my MtlPietRenderer so it feels redundant to have both a `graphite::MtlPietRenderer` and a `piet::MtlRenderer`\n\nAs you pointed out we could certainly remove src/gpu/piet/Renderer.* and move all that logic here to PietRenderer and MtlPietRenderer and call the pgpu API functions here directly (or perhaps go through an unspecialized `piet::RendererBase`). I personally like keeping all direct piet C API calls outside graphite but I\u0027m not too strongly attached to the idea if you think merging them here would be better.",
      "parentUuid": "bc3a6388_e655bf97",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 32,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 32,
        "endChar": 38
      },
